{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development would result in inadequate living conditions to prospective occupants on the site, by having insufficient gross internal floor area and storage space, which is contrary to the following planning policies: &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Technical Housing Standards (Nationally Described Space Standard) (as amended) (DCLG, March 2015);&lt;br&gt;- Policy D6 of the London Plan (March 2021);&lt;br&gt;- London Plan Guidance: Housing Design Standards (June 2023)&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development, by reason of siting, bulk and mass, would create an increased sense of closure at this prominent corner plot. It would also unbalance the symmetry and uniformity of the terrace row, and consequently constitute harm to the street scene and local character. As such, the proposal is contrary to:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;-&amp;nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2023);&lt;br&gt;- Policy D4 of the London Plan (June 2021);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Policies SP2 and DMD1 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development, by reason of siting and appearance, would result in detrimental harm to the neighbouring property. Specifically, the proposed development would result in a material loss of sunlight/daylight and outlook, and unacceptable overshadowing and overbearing impacts on 44 Blackborne Road, therefore constituting unneighbourly development and contrary to: &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2023);&lt;br&gt;- Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021); &lt;br&gt;- Policies SP2 and DMD1 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development, by reasons of not being able to provide sufficient car parking space due to its proximity to the junction between Blackborne Road and Manning Road, and no cycle parking proposed, would potentially increase parking pressure to the surroundings and discourage sustainable travel options, which is considered unacceptable from the perspective of transport/highway, therefore contrary to the following policies:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;-&amp;nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2023);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Policies T5 and T6 of the London Plan (March 2021);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;London Plan Guidance: Housing Design Standards (GLA, June 2023);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Policies DMT2 and DMT3 of the&amp;nbsp;London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
